Ladybird gives the platform the media session that most recently
appeared or started playing, across all tabs, and keeps a paused
one there until another plays or its page goes away. Chromium makes
the same choice in AudioFocusManager::MaybeUpdateActiveSession(),
and Gecko’s ControllerManager::UpdateMainControllerIfNeeded()
makes a controller that starts playing the main one. WebKit’s
GPUProcess::recomputeNowPlayingOwner() prefers the largest
main-content media instead.

When the metadata has no title, Ladybird shows the tab’s title,
as Chromium’s MediaSessionImpl::BuildMetadata() and Gecko’s
MediaStatusManager::FillMissingTitleAndArtworkIfNeeded() do. WebKit
uses the media element’s title attribute, then the document title
(HTMLMediaElement::mediaSessionTitle()).

Ladybird never lets a declared playbackState move a session ahead,
as the spec requires; Chromium’s audio-focus stack and Gecko’s main
controller also change only when media starts playing.

Ladybird publishes a private window’s session with the title “Ladybird”
and no artist, album, or artwork. Chromium replaces an incognito
session’s metadata with a private-playback title (MediaSessionClient::
ShouldHideMetadata()), Gecko withholds a private window’s metadata
(MediaStatusManager::IsInPrivateBrowsing()), and WebKit clears it for an
ephemeral session (MediaElementSession::computeNowPlayingInfo()).

These comparisons come from reading each engine’s source, at Chromium
8658d682eac5, WebKit 047a16c96ccd, and Gecko 06fa2674be3d: What an
engine hands the OS media controls isn’t observable from page script, so
it couldn’t be compared by running them.
